      "repeated_issue_lines": [
        [
          "[ERROR] `\"Ref:\" + $in.0.text` \u2014 CTL2 string concatenation renders a null operand as the literal text `null`; it does not use an empty string. Because text is nullable, a permitted null row emits `Ref:null`, whereas the stated display value for that row is exactly `Ref:`.",
          8
        ],
        [
          "[ERROR] `3000000000` \u2014 An unsuffixed whole-number literal is parsed as a 32-bit integer before assignment is considered. This value exceeds 2147483647, so assigning it to a long output does not rescue the literal; the parser reports an out-of-range integer and compilation fails.",
          7
        ],
        [
          "[ERROR] `$out.0.normalized_keys` \u2014 The output metadata declares `normalized_key` in the singular and has no `normalized_keys` field. CTL2 checks named output fields against port 0 metadata when compiling, so this assignment has no target and fails before any trimmed identifier can be emitted.",
          7
        ],
        [
          "[WARNING] `isnull($in.0.text) || $in.0.text == null` \u2014 Both the lowercase `isnull()` predicate and equality with null are valid, null-safe tests for this nullable string. They always agree, so the OR returns the requested missing_text value. Repeating the test is suspicious but creates no incorrect row or exception.",
          5
        ],
        [
          "[WARNING] `str2date(\"2025-01-01\", \"yyyy-MM-dd\")` \u2014 This exact constant matches its stated format, so parsing it yields the required fixed date on every row. CTL2 also supports a native date literal for constants. The conversion is valid, but it needlessly invokes a parser and obscures that the value never varies.",
          5
        ],
        [
          "[WARNING] `nvl($in.0.settlement_amount, 0D)` \u2014 The settlement_amount input is declared non-null, and both the source and fallback are decimal-compatible. Every permitted row therefore takes the original settlement_amount and emits the requested copy. The zero fallback is valid CTL2 but suggests a null policy that this metadata never needs.",
          5
        ],
        [
          "[ERROR] `isNull($in.0.optional_cap)` \u2014 The camelCase `isNull()` overload checks a field in a record and requires two arguments: the record plus its field name or index. This single decimal argument matches neither overload, so CTL2 cannot compile the transform. The one-value predicate is lowercase `isnull(...)`.",
          3
        ],
        [
          "[ERROR] `$in.0.optional_cap > 100D` \u2014 The input metadata explicitly permits a null optional_cap. CTL2 ordered comparisons throw when either operand is null; they do not return false. A permitted row with optional_cap null therefore raises a runtime exception instead of emitting the required false flag.",
          3
        ],
        [
          "[ERROR] `$in.0.item_count / 2` \u2014 Both operands are integers, so division truncates before the result is widened for the decimal output field. For a permitted row with item_count 3, the expression computes 1 and emits 1 rather than the required 1.5.",
          3
        ],
        [
          "[ERROR] `if($in.0.seat_count > 0, true, false)` \u2014 `if` is CTL2 control-flow syntax, not a three-argument expression function. The compiler parses this position as an invalid if statement and stops. The positive-seat_count condition itself is valid; its surrounding function-call form is the defect.",
          3
        ],
        [
          "[ERROR] `$in.0.sample_count++` \u2014 CTL2 allows increment on writable output fields or local variables, but an input record field is read-only. Attempting to mutate sample_count through `$in.0` is rejected at compilation. The later assignment cannot implement the required plus-one value from valid code.",
          3
        ],
        [
          "[ERROR] `$out.0.amount_as_int = $in.0.settlement_amount` \u2014 The source field has decimal type while amount_as_int is integer metadata. CTL2 permits numeric widening but does not silently narrow decimal to integer on assignment. The statement therefore has a compile-time type mismatch, even for rows whose settlement_amount happens to be whole.",
          3
        ],
        [
          "[ERROR] `$in.0.settlement_amount / $in.0.cycle_count` \u2014 Amount is decimal and cycle_count is a non-null integer that may still be zero. Decimal division by zero throws rather than yielding the requested null sentinel. For a permitted row with cycle_count 0, the transform raises an exception before average_amount is emitted.",
          3
        ],
        [
          "[ERROR] `date2num(str2date($in.0.observed_date_text, \"yyyy-MM-dd\"))` \u2014 Parsing the ISO text can produce a date, but `date2num()` requires a second, unquoted unit such as `day`. With only the date argument, no overload applies and the transform fails to compile before date_day can be assigned.",
          2
        ],
        [
          "[ERROR] `$in.0.reserve_value > 100D` \u2014 The input metadata explicitly permits a null reserve_value. CTL2 ordered comparisons throw when either operand is null; they do not return false. A permitted row with reserve_value null therefore raises a runtime exception instead of emitting the required false flag.",
          2
        ]
      ]
